# name: test/sql/bugs/147_is_referenced_self.test
# description: #147 -- a definition's own name node must not count as a reference to it
# group: [bugs]

# THE DEFECT
#
# `:is-referenced` matched every named definition, so `.func:not(:is-referenced)`
# -- which docs/reference/css-pseudo-classes.md presents as THE dead-code query
# -- always returned 0 rows. Measured on build/release/duckdb at 30704be9:
#
#   .fn                      20     .fn:is-referenced       20
#   .fn:not(:is-referenced)   0
#
# The cause is that a definition's own name is a separate node, it is a DIRECT
# CHILD of the definition, and it carries NAME_REFERENCE. Measured on
# test/data/python/sample_app.py:
#
#   node_id  type                 name       is_def  is_ref  parent_id
#   761      function_definition  transform  true    false   --
#   763      identifier           transform  false   true    761
#
# The `:is-referenced` arm looked for any NAME_REFERENCE node with the same name
# and a different node_id, so node 763 answered for node 761 and every
# definition referenced itself.
#
# THE RULE, and why it is the subtree and not just that child
#
# References from inside the definition's OWN SUBTREE do not count. Teague ruled
# on 2026-11-05 that a recursive self-call is not "being referenced", because the
# documented purpose is dead-code detection and a function called only by itself
# is dead. The subtree range also does not depend on WHICH node carries the
# definition flag, which matters while #276 is open -- there container and
# declarator nodes both carry NAME_DEFINITION.
#
# `recurse` in the fixture is the case that distinguishes the two candidate
# fixes. Excluding only the direct name child leaves it referenced by its own
# recursive call; excluding the subtree does not.
#
# NOT A DIVERGENCE TO FIX: ast_dead_code() answers a different question and is
# allowed to disagree here. It matches on name alone, collects every call name
# including a self-call, and excludes `main`, `setup`, `teardown`, `init`,
# `constructor` and dunder names outright (src/sql_macros/tree_navigation.sql).
# Teague confirmed on 2026-11-05 that these are different things.

require sitting_duck

statement ok
LOAD sitting_duck;

# =============================================================================
# 1. THE FIX -- exactly which definitions are referenced
#
# Two ast_select calls carry the whole discrimination, as a partition of .fn.
# Each selector call costs ~13 s of planning on the DuckDB v2.0 line
# (duckdb/duckdb#26036), so the names are asserted as one aggregate rather than
# one query per name.
#
# Referenced: inner_same, level2 and level3 are each named from OUTSIDE their own
# subtree -- `return inner_same`, and the nested calls in the depth chain.
# =============================================================================

query I
SELECT string_agg(name, ',' ORDER BY name)
FROM ast_select('test/data/callgraph/edge_cases.py', '.fn:is-referenced');
----
inner_same,level2,level3

# The complement, which is what #147 reported as permanently empty. `recurse`
# appears here: its only reference is its own recursive call.
query I
SELECT string_agg(name, ',' ORDER BY name)
FROM ast_select('test/data/callgraph/edge_cases.py', '.fn:not(:is-referenced)');
----
direct_and_nested,level1,method,method_lambda,recurse,twin_a,twin_b,with_comprehension

# =============================================================================
# 2. CONTROL -- the flag itself is untouched
#
# The fix belongs in the selector, not in the extractor. A definition's own name
# node must KEEP NAME_REFERENCE, because ast_find_references and the resolver
# read it. If a future change "fixes" #147 by clearing that flag instead, this
# assertion fails and names the reason.
# =============================================================================

query IIII
SELECT type, name, is_name_definition(flags), is_name_reference(flags)
FROM read_ast('test/data/python/sample_app.py')
WHERE node_id IN (761, 763) ORDER BY node_id;
----
function_definition	transform	true	false
identifier	transform	false	true

# The own-name node is a direct child of its definition, which is the fact the
# subtree range relies on.
query I
SELECT r.parent_id = d.node_id
FROM read_ast('test/data/python/sample_app.py') d, read_ast('test/data/python/sample_app.py') r
WHERE d.node_id = 761 AND r.node_id = 763;
----
true

# =============================================================================
# 3. CONTROL -- a definition referenced from elsewhere in the file still matches
#
# Guards the over-correction: excluding the subtree must not exclude references
# that merely sit in another definition. `transform` is called by `main`, and
# `validate_email` by `create_user`, both from outside their own subtrees.
# =============================================================================

query I
SELECT count(*) FROM read_ast('test/data/python/sample_app.py') d
WHERE d.type = 'function_definition' AND d.name IN ('transform', 'validate_email')
  AND EXISTS (
      SELECT 1 FROM read_ast('test/data/python/sample_app.py') r
      WHERE r.file_path = d.file_path AND is_name_reference(r.flags) AND r.name = d.name
        AND NOT (r.node_id > d.node_id AND r.node_id <= d.node_id + d.descendant_count)
  );
----
2
